#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:81
msgid ""
"Tcl module files generated with default options can be evaluated by both "
"Environment Modules and Lmod. Options relying on features that only "
"Environment Modules provides, such as :ref:`module variants <module-"
"variants>`, are disabled by default, and their documentation states the "
"minimum Environment Modules version they require."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:87
msgid "Flat and hierarchical module files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:89
msgid ""
"In **flat** (non-hierarchical) mode, all module files are installed in a "
"single directory. Default settings are such that:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:92
msgid ""
"Each module name encodes the package version, compiler, and a hash to "
"distinguish builds with different configurations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:93
msgid ""
"Every installed package has a visible module, and dependencies are made "
"available through :ref:`autoloading <autoloading-dependencies>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:95
msgid ""
"The naming scheme can be customized as the :ref:`modules-projections` "
"section shows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:97
msgid ""
"In **hierarchical** mode, module files are organized into layers: ``Core``, "
"``Compiler``, and optionally additional layers such as ``mpi``, ``lapack``, "
"or ``python``. Loading a compiler module extends ``MODULEPATH`` to reveal "
"the packages built with that compiler. Similarly, loading an MPI module "
"reveals the MPI-dependent packages, and so on. Users are therefore only "
"offered packages that are coherent with their loaded environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:102
msgid ""
"Flat mode is the default for ``tcl``, while hierarchical mode is the default "
"for ``lmod``, but either generator supports either mode. Set ``hierarchical: "
"true`` under the ``tcl`` key to enable hierarchical Tcl modules, or "
"``hierarchical: false`` under the ``lmod`` key to generate flat Lua modules. "
"See :ref:`hierarchical-modules` for the full configuration options and tool-"
"version requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:107
msgid ""
"Spack ships with sensible defaults for the generation of module files, but "
"you can customize many aspects of it to accommodate package or site specific "
"needs. In general you can override or extend the default behavior by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:110
msgid "overriding certain callback APIs in the Python packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:111
msgid "writing specific rules in the ``modules.yaml`` configuration file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:112
msgid "writing your own templates to override or extend the defaults"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:114
msgid ""
"Overriding callback APIs lets you express changes in the run-time "
"environment that are needed to use the installed software properly, e.g. "
"injecting variables from language interpreters into their extensions. "
"Configuration rules and custom templates instead permit you to fine-tune the "
"filesystem layout, content and creation of module files to meet site-"
"specific conventions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:122
msgid "Configuration structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:124
msgid ""
"The configuration files that control module generation behavior are named "
"``modules.yaml``. The default configuration looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:127
msgid ""
"# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"# This is the default configuration for Spack's module file generation.\n"
"#\n"
"# Settings here are versioned with Spack and are intended to provide\n"
"# sensible defaults out of the box. Spack maintainers should edit this\n"
"# file to keep it current.\n"
"#\n"
"# Users can override these settings by editing the following files.\n"
"#\n"
"# Per-spack-instance settings (overrides defaults):\n"
"#   $SPACK_ROOT/etc/spack/modules.yaml\n"
"#\n"
"# Per-user settings (overrides default and site settings):\n"
"#   ~/.spack/modules.yaml\n"
"# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n"
"modules:\n"
"  # This maps paths in the package install prefix to environment variables\n"
"  # they should be added to. For example, <prefix>/bin should be in PATH.\n"
"  prefix_inspections:\n"
"    ./bin:\n"
"      - PATH\n"
"    ./man:\n"
"      - MANPATH\n"
"    ./share/man:\n"
"      - MANPATH\n"
"    ./share/aclocal:\n"
"      - ACLOCAL_PATH\n"
"    ./lib/pkgconfig:\n"
"      - PKG_CONFIG_PATH\n"
"    ./lib64/pkgconfig:\n"
"      - PKG_CONFIG_PATH\n"
"    ./share/pkgconfig:\n"
"      - PKG_CONFIG_PATH\n"
"    ./:\n"
"      - CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH\n"
"\n"
"  # These are configurations for the module set named \"default\"\n"
"  default:\n"
"    # Where to install modules\n"
"    roots:\n"
"     tcl:    $spack/share/spack/modules\n"
"     lmod:   $spack/share/spack/lmod\n"
"    # What type of modules to use (\"tcl\" and/or \"lmod\")\n"
"    enable: []\n"
"\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      all:\n"
"        autoload: direct\n"
"\n"
"    # Default configurations if lmod is enabled\n"
"    lmod:\n"
"      all:\n"
"        autoload: direct\n"
"      hierarchy:\n"
"        - mpi\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:130
msgid ""
"You can define one or more **module sets**, each of which can be configured "
"separately with regard to install location, naming scheme, inclusion and "
"exclusion, autoloading, et cetera."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:132
msgid ""
"The default module set is aptly named ``default``. All :ref:`Spack commands "
"that operate on modules <maintaining-module-files>` apply to the ``default`` "
"module set, unless another module set is specified explicitly (with the ``--"
"name`` flag)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:135
msgid ""
"To automatically generate module files when packages are installed, add the "
"desired module systems to the ``enable`` list:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:137
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    enable:\n"
"    - tcl\n"
"    - lmod"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:145
msgid ""
"The module files of the packages that a Spack command installs are written "
"when that command ends. While a command is still running, a package it has "
"already installed may not have a module file yet, also when another command "
"uses that package as a dependency. If a command is killed before it ends, "
"generate the missing module files with ``spack module tcl refresh`` or "
"``spack module lmod refresh``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:149
msgid ""
"Spack writes each module file to a temporary file in the same directory, "
"then renames it over the previous version, so a module system never reads a "
"partially written file. Creating, rewriting or removing a module file "
"therefore requires write permission on the directory that contains it. The "
"directories that Spack creates for module files follow the umask, and get in "
"addition the group and the write access that the :ref:`package permissions "
"<package_permissions>` set for ``all``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:153
msgid ""
"You can configure the behavior of either module system separately, under a "
"key corresponding to the generator being customized:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:155
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      # contains environment modules specific customizations\n"
"    lmod:\n"
"      # contains lmod specific customizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:164
msgid ""
"In general, the configuration options that you can use in ``modules.yaml`` "
"will either change the layout of the module files on the filesystem, or they "
"will affect their content. For the latter point it is possible to "
"use :ref:`spec patterns <sec-specs>` to fine-tune the set of packages on "
"which the modifications should be applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:167
msgid ""
"The default module root can be overridden for any module set by changing the "
"``roots`` key of the configuration. For example, to install ``tcl`` modules "
"to a custom path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:170
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    roots:\n"
"      tcl: /path/to/install/tcl/modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:177
msgid ""
"By default, an architecture-specific directory is added to the root "
"directory. A module set may override that behavior by setting the "
"``arch_folder`` config value to ``false``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:180
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    roots:\n"
"      tcl: /path/to/install/tcl/modules\n"
"    arch_folder: false"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:188
msgid ""
"Spack processes that change module files take an exclusive lock on the "
"``.spack-lock`` file in the module root, so that concurrent commands like "
"``spack install`` and ``spack uninstall`` write the files they share one at "
"a time. When the module root is shared by several users, the lock file must "
"be writable by all of them: Spack creates it with the group and the write "
"access configured in ``packages:all:permissions``. These are applied again "
"to the module root and to the lock file by ``spack module <type> refresh --"
"delete-tree``, when run by the user who owns them. A process waits for the "
"lock up to ``config:db_lock_timeout`` seconds, then warns that it did not "
"update its module files. Setting ``config:locks`` to ``false`` disables the "
"lock."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:195
msgid "Module layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:197
msgid ""
"By default, Spack encodes the package version, compiler, and a short hash "
"into every module name to distinguish builds with different configurations. "
"The options in this section let you change where module files are placed on "
"the filesystem, how they are named, whether to arrange them in a hierarchy, "
"whether they define package variants, and which version is the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:205
msgid "Naming and projections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:207
msgid ""
"Spack provides three options to make module names shorter and easier to "
"read. The first is a global setting to adjust the hash length. It accepts "
"values from 0 to 32 (default: 7):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:211
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      hash_length: 7"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:218
msgid ""
"A hash length of 0 removes the hash completely, making the module name more "
"readable, but also more subject to naming conflicts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:220
msgid ""
"The second option is ``suffixes``, which appends a string to the names of "
"modules matching a spec. For example, the following configuration adds a "
"``python3.12`` suffix to any package built with Python 3.12:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:223
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      all:\n"
"        suffixes:\n"
"          ^python@3: \"python{^python.version.up_to_2}\"\n"
"          ^openblas: \"openblas\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:233
msgid ""
"This is useful to know which version of Python a set of Python extensions is "
"associated with. Likewise, the ``openblas`` string is attached to any "
"program that has ``openblas`` in the spec, most likely via the ``+blas`` "
"variant specification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:236
msgid ""
"The third, and most flexible, option is to change the entire naming "
"convention using the projections format covered in :ref:`view_projections`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:238
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      projections:\n"
"        all: \"{name}/{version}-{compiler.name}-{compiler.version}-module\"\n"
"        ^mpi: \"{name}/{version}-{^mpi.name}-{^mpi.version}-{compiler.name}-"
"{compiler.version}-module\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:247
msgid "Note that the default projection for module files differs by mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:249
msgid "In hierarchical mode it is ``{name}/{version}``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:250
msgid ""
"In flat mode it is ``{name}/{version}-{compiler.name}-{compiler.version}``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:255
msgid "Hierarchical module files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:257
msgid "Three keys control the shape of the hierarchy:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:259
msgid ""
"``core_compilers`` lists the compilers whose packages go in the ``Core`` "
"layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:260
msgid ""
"``core_specs`` lists any additional packages that should also go in "
"``Core``, bypassing the normal hierarchy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:261
msgid ""
"``hierarchy`` lists the virtual packages that define the additional layers "
"beyond ``Core`` and ``Compiler``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:263
msgid ""
"The configuration below generates a hierarchy in which the ``python``, "
"``lapack``, and ``mpi`` layers can be switched independently:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:265
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    lmod:\n"
"      core_compilers:\n"
"      - \"gcc@13\"\n"
"      core_specs:\n"
"      - \"r\"\n"
"      hierarchy:\n"
"      - \"mpi\"\n"
"      - \"lapack\"\n"
"      - \"python\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:279
msgid ""
"For ``tcl``, whose default layout is flat, add ``hierarchical: true`` to the "
"same configuration (this requires either Environment Modules >= 5.6 or Lmod "
">= 8.7.31)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:281
msgid ""
"Note that ``core_specs`` bypasses the hierarchy that allows the module tool "
"to safely switch between coherent software stacks, so the user is "
"responsible for maintaining consistency among those packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:286
msgid "Package variants and module files for several installations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:290
msgid ""
"Module files define variants with the `variant`_ command of `Environment "
"Modules`_. This requires version 5.1 or newer, with the `advanced module "
"version specifiers`_ configuration option enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:293
msgid ""
"Several installations of the same package version can be written to one Tcl "
"module file, and ``module load`` selects one of them by "
"its :ref:`variants<basic-variants>`. This is enabled by the ``variants`` key "
"under the ``tcl`` module configuration, which accepts the values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:296
msgid "``none`` (default): do not define variants in module files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:297
msgid ""
"``all``: define all variants from the installed spec in the module file, "
"except those reserved by Spack such as ``patches`` or ``dev_path``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:306
msgid ""
"For example, with two installations of ``zlib@1.3.2`` that differ only in "
"the ``shared`` variant, and the following configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:308
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      variants: all"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:315
msgid ""
"Spack writes a single ``zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0`` module file, and ``spack "
"module tcl find`` prints the variants of each installation after the module "
"name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:317
msgid ""
"$ spack module tcl find zlib~shared\n"
"zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0+optimize+pic~shared build_system=makefile "
"hash=ickxcoy\n"
"$ spack module tcl find zlib+shared\n"
"zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0+optimize+pic+shared build_system=makefile hash=vzg6net"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:324
msgid ""
"Users select an installation by stating its variants on the ``module load`` "
"command line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:326
msgid ""
"$ module load -v zlib +shared\n"
"Loading zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0{build_system=makefile:hash=vzg6net:+optimize:"
"+pic:+shared}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:331
msgid ""
"Stating all the variants is not needed, and several installations may match "
"the ones stated. ``module load`` then selects the one that was installed "
"first. Here ``zlib ~shared`` was installed before ``zlib +shared``, so a "
"plain ``module load zlib`` loads it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:335
msgid ""
"$ module load -v zlib\n"
"Loading zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0{build_system=makefile:hash=ickxcoy:+optimize:"
"+pic:-shared}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:340
msgid ""
"Installing another ``zlib@1.3.2`` later does not change what these commands "
"load. The order is the one in which the installations were completed, "
"whether they were requested or installed as dependencies. An installation "
"built as a dependency of another installation of the same package version is "
"thus selected before it. To have an installation selected before the older "
"ones, list it under ``defaults``, see `Default module versions`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:345
msgid ""
"If the stated variants do not match any installation, the error lists the "
"installed configurations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:347
msgid ""
"$ module load zlib -pic +shared\n"
"Loading zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0{-pic:+shared}\n"
"  ERROR: Specified package is not installed, available packages for this "
"version are:\n"
"    * \"build_system=makefile +optimize +pic ~shared hash=ickxcoy\"\n"
"    * \"build_system=makefile +optimize +pic +shared hash=vzg6net\"\n"
"  ERROR: Module evaluation aborted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:356
msgid ""
"After setting or unsetting ``variants`` on an existing module tree, "
"regenerate it with ``spack module tcl refresh --delete-tree -y``. A plain "
"``refresh`` leaves the module files written with the previous setting in "
"place, and ``module load`` may pick one of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:360
msgid "Hashes and dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:361
msgid ""
"With ``variants: all``, the hash moves from the module name to a ``hash`` "
"variant. The ``hash_length`` option is then ignored, and Spack warns if it "
"is set to a non-zero value. Two installations in the same module file may "
"have the same variants, when they differ only by their dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:365
msgid ""
"$ spack install hdf5@1.14 ^openmpi\n"
"$ spack install hdf5@1.14 ^mpich"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:370
msgid ""
"The ``hash`` variant tells them apart: ``spack module tcl find`` prints it "
"for an installation, and ``module load`` selects by it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:372
msgid ""
"$ spack module tcl find hdf5 ^mpich\n"
"$ module load hdf5 hash=hyob6r5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:377
msgid ""
"The ``depends-on`` lines of dependent module files state the ``hash`` "
"variant only. A dependent thus keeps loading the installation it was built "
"against, whatever is later added to or removed from the module file of its "
"dependency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:381
msgid "Differences between Spack and Environment Modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:382
msgid ""
"The ``module`` command reads variants with the syntax of Environment "
"Modules, which differs from the Spack spec syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:387
msgid "Spack spec"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:388
msgid "``module`` command line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:389
msgid "``+man``, ``~man``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:390
msgid "``+man``, ``~man`` or ``-man``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:391
msgid "``libs=shared,static``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:392
msgid "``libs=shared_static``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:393
msgid "``languages=c,c++``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:394
msgid "``languages=c_cxx``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:395
msgid "``device=ch3:sock``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:396
msgid "``device=ch3_sock``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:397
msgid "``use_vtkm=on``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:398
msgid "``use_vtkm=on_``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:399
msgid "``@2.53.0``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:400
msgid "``@2.53.0-gcc-15.2.1``, the version part of the module name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:401
msgid "``%gcc``, ``^zlib``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:402
msgid "not supported, read as names of other modules to load"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:404
msgid ""
"A shell expands a word starting with ``~`` to a home directory, so write ``-"
"shared``, or append the boolean variants to the module name as in ``zlib/"
"1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0~shared``. ``spack module tcl find`` and ``spack module tcl "
"loads`` print module names in the latter form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:407
msgid ""
"Some variants exist only under a condition: ``python`` has a ``tix`` variant "
"only when built with ``+tkinter``. An installation that does not have the "
"variant is selected as if the variant were disabled. For example, with "
"``python +tkinter +tix`` and ``python ~tkinter`` in the same module file, "
"``module load python ~tix`` loads ``python ~tkinter``. For a variant that "
"takes a value, the value to state is ``none``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:412
msgid "Only one installation of a module file can be loaded at a time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:416
msgid ""
"``module save`` does not record the variants of the installations loaded, so "
"``module restore`` loads the ones a plain ``module load`` selects. To record "
"them, enable the `collection_pin_version`_ configuration option of "
"Environment Modules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:419
msgid ""
"$ module config collection_pin_version 1\n"
"$ module load zlib +shared\n"
"$ module save mywork\n"
"$ module saveshow mywork\n"
"module use --append /home/user/spack/share/spack/modules/linux-ubuntu24.04-"
"alderlake\n"
"module load zlib/1.3.2-gcc-13.3.0 build_system=makefile hash=vzg6net "
"+optimize +pic +shared"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:429
msgid "Maintaining the module files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:430
msgid ""
"Spack writes a module file again when a command installs or uninstalls one "
"of its installations, and deletes it when none remain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:432
msgid ""
"To leave an installation out of a module file, use the ``exclude`` option. "
"``spack module tcl rm`` does not last: the installation is still in the "
"database, and is written back the next time the module file is. Until then, "
"``spack module tcl find`` and ``spack module tcl loads`` still print a "
"module name for it, which ``module load`` refuses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:436
msgid ""
"The ``spack module tcl`` subcommands account for the installations in the "
"same module file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:441
msgid "Command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:442
msgid "With several installations in one module file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:443
msgid "``find``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:444
msgid ""
"prints the module name without variants when the spec matches several of them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:445
msgid "``loads``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:446
msgid "prints one load line per module file, and comments out the others"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:447
msgid "``rm``, ``refresh``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:448
msgid "also list the other installations in the module files they write"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:449
msgid "``setdefault``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:450
msgid "refuses, as the ``default`` symlink would point to all of them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:453
msgid "Projections and templates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:454
msgid ""
"Installations are in the same module file when they have the same version "
"and the same :ref:`projected<modules-projections>` name. Add a token to the "
"projection, such as ``{^mpi.name}``, to keep them in separate module files. "
"A projection without ``{version}`` gives different versions the same name, "
"and ``spack module tcl refresh`` reports a name clash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:458
msgid ""
"With ``variants: all``, a module file is rendered from a single template, so "
"set the ``template`` option for a whole package or for a package version. "
"Spack reports a configuration error if the installations of a module file "
"are configured with different templates, and ``spack audit configs`` flags a "
"``template`` set under any other constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:462
msgid "Default module versions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:464
msgid ""
"To pin a specific version as the default, add a ``defaults`` key to your "
"modules configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:466
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  my-module-set:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      defaults:\n"
"      - gcc@10.2.1\n"
"      - hdf5@1.2.10+mpi+hl%gcc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:475
msgid ""
"The spec can be as specific as needed. If multiple packages in the same "
"directory match, the last one generated wins. In a module file :ref:`holding "
"several installations<module-variants-folding>`, the installation matching a "
"default is listed first, so a plain ``module load`` selects it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:480
msgid "Module content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:482
msgid ""
"When a user loads a module, the module system executes the module file, "
"which sets environment variables and optionally loads dependencies. Spack "
"populates module files from three sources:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:485
msgid "Prefix inspections derived automatically from the installation layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:486
msgid "Rules in ``modules.yaml`` applied to matching packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:487
msgid "Callbacks defined in ``package.py`` for package-specific logic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:489
msgid ""
"The options in this section let you control which dependencies are "
"autoloaded, what environment variables are set and for which packages, and "
"which module files are generated at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:496
msgid "Autoloading and hiding dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:498
msgid ""
"Each package's module file sets only the environment variables for that "
"package itself. Autoloading handles the rest: when a module is loaded, Spack "
"can automatically load the modules for its dependencies, so users do not "
"need to track them manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:501
msgid ""
"The ``autoload`` key controls the autoloading behavior, and can take one of "
"these values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:503
msgid "``none``: no autoloading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:504
msgid "``run``: autoload direct *run* type dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:505
msgid "``direct``: autoload direct *link and run* type dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:506
msgid "``all``: autoload all dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:508
msgid ""
"``direct`` is the most correct choice, but ``run`` is often sufficient and "
"may be faster to load."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:510
msgid ""
"``hide_implicits: true`` limits ``module avail`` to only the packages you "
"explicitly installed, while still autoloading hidden dependencies. We "
"recommend enabling both options together:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:513
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      hide_implicits: true\n"
"      all:\n"
"        autoload: direct # or 'run'\n"
"    lmod:\n"
"      hide_implicits: true\n"
"      all:\n"
"        autoload: direct # or 'run'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:526
msgid ""
"This ensures all runtime variables are set correctly, limits visible modules "
"to what users explicitly installed, and makes shorter module names easier to "
"use without conflicts (see :ref:`modules-projections`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:529
msgid "Module conflicts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:531
msgid ""
"Tcl and Lua modules allow for explicit conflicts between module files. Using "
"the ``conflict`` key under a spec pattern, you can prevent two incompatible "
"modules from being loaded at the same time:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:534
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      all:\n"
"        conflict:\n"
"        - \"{name}\"\n"
"        - \"intel/14.0.1\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:544
msgid ""
"This prevents loading any other version of the same package (via ``{name}``) "
"or ``intel/14.0.1``. For Lmod, and for Environment Modules versions prior to "
"4.2, the conflict must be expressed on both module files conflicting with "
"each other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:550
msgid "Global environment modifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:552
msgid ""
"Spack automatically adds environment variable modifications to every module "
"file by inspecting the installation prefix. The ``prefix_inspections`` "
"section maps relative paths inside the prefix to the environment variables "
"that should be updated with them. In the configuration, paths are written "
"relative to the installation prefix, so ``./bin`` means ``<prefix>/bin``, "
"and ``./`` means ``<prefix>`` itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:556
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  prefix_inspections:\n"
"    ./bin:\n"
"    - PATH\n"
"    ./man:\n"
"    - MANPATH\n"
"    ./:\n"
"    - CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:567
msgid ""
"Unlike other module settings, ``prefix_inspections`` placed at the top "
"``modules`` level applies to all module sets at once. An inspection is only "
"applied when the corresponding subdirectory exists in the installation "
"prefix. The table below lists the default inspections:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:572
msgid "Path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:572
msgid "Environment variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:574
msgid "``<prefix>/bin``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:574
msgid "``PATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:576
msgid "``<prefix>/man``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:576 ../../../module_file_support.rst:578
msgid "``MANPATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:578
msgid "``<prefix>/share/man``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:580
msgid "``<prefix>/share/aclocal``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:580
msgid "``ACLOCAL_PATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:582
msgid "``<prefix>/lib/pkgconfig``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:582 ../../../module_file_support.rst:584
#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:586
msgid "``PKG_CONFIG_PATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:584
msgid "``<prefix>/lib64/pkgconfig``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:586
msgid "``<prefix>/share/pkgconfig``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:588
msgid "``<prefix>``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:588
msgid "``CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:590
msgid "``<prefix>/lib`` (macOS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:590 ../../../module_file_support.rst:592
msgid "``DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:592
msgid "``<prefix>/lib64`` (macOS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:595
msgid ""
"On Linux, ``LD_LIBRARY_PATH`` is omitted: Spack packages embed RPATHs, "
"making it unnecessary, and setting it globally affects system executables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:597
msgid "To suppress a variable from all module files, use ``exclude_env_vars``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:599
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      all:\n"
"        filter:\n"
"          exclude_env_vars: [\"CPATH\", \"LIBRARY_PATH\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:614
msgid "Selective environment modifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:616
msgid ""
"The ``environment`` key lets you set, prepend, or unset variables for any "
"set of packages matching a spec pattern. This is useful when you want to "
"apply a change to many packages at once, or when you prefer to keep the "
"logic in configuration rather than in :ref:`package.py <override-api-calls-"
"in-package-py>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:619
msgid ""
"The following example shows the four main pattern forms and the operations "
"available under ``environment``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:621
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      # Selects every package\n"
"      all:\n"
"        environment:\n"
"          set:\n"
"            BAR: \"bar\"\n"
"      # Selects MPI-dependent packages. The :: clears previously matched "
"rules\n"
"      ^mpi::\n"
"        environment:\n"
"          set:\n"
"            BAR: \"baz\"\n"
"      # Selects any zlib package\n"
"      zlib:\n"
"        environment:\n"
"          prepend_path:\n"
"            LD_LIBRARY_PATH: \"{prefix}/lib\"\n"
"      # Selects zlib compiled with gcc@13\n"
"      zlib%gcc@13:\n"
"        environment:\n"
"          unset:\n"
"          - FOOBAR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:647
msgid ""
"Each rule selects a set of packages and applies the corresponding "
"modifications:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:649
msgid "``all``: sets ``BAR=bar`` for every module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:650
msgid ""
"For specs matching ``^mpi::``: ``BAR`` is overridden to ``baz``. The "
"trailing ``::`` clears all previously matched rules, so the ``all`` block's "
"``BAR=bar`` does not carry over."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:652
msgid "``zlib``: prepends ``{prefix}/lib`` to ``LD_LIBRARY_PATH``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:653
msgid "``zlib%gcc@13``: unsets ``FOOBAR``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:655
msgid ""
"The ``all`` block is always evaluated first regardless of where it appears "
"in the file. All other rules are evaluated top to bottom. As general advice, "
"set only the variables a package actually needs: unexpected interactions "
"with other tools are hard to debug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:662
msgid "Package-specific environment modifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:664
msgid ""
"Two methods in ``package.py`` let you inject environment modifications "
"directly into module files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:666
msgid ""
"``setup_run_environment`` sets variables based on the package's own spec:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:668
msgid ""
"def setup_run_environment(self, env: EnvironmentModifications) -> None:\n"
"    if self.spec.satisfies(\"+foo\"):\n"
"        env.set(\"FOO\", \"bar\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:674
msgid ""
"``setup_dependent_run_environment`` allows a package to set variables in the "
"module files of its dependents. This is used in interpreter packages such as "
"``python``, ``r``, and ``perl``. When a dependent is built, the interpreter "
"prepends the dependent's prefix to its search path (``PYTHONPATH``, "
"``R_LIBS``, or ``PERL5LIB``) so the interpreter can locate it at runtime. A "
"simplified ``python`` package looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:679
msgid ""
"def setup_dependent_run_environment(\n"
"    self, env: EnvironmentModifications, dependent_spec: Spec\n"
") -> None:\n"
"    if dependent_spec.package.extends(self.spec):\n"
"        env.prepend_path(\"PYTHONPATH\", dependent_spec.prefix.lib.python)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:687
msgid ""
"This ensures every Python extension has its library directory on "
"``PYTHONPATH`` without repeating the logic in each extension's "
"``setup_run_environment``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:690
msgid "Modules relative to environment views"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:692
msgid ""
"When a Spack :ref:`environment <environments>` uses a view, you can "
"configure a module set to generate module files relative to the view paths "
"instead of the install tree. This requires a view whose :ref:`projection "
"<configuring_environment_views>` assigns each package a unique subdirectory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:695
msgid "The ``use_view`` key controls this behavior:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:697
msgid "``true``: use the default view of the environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:698
msgid ""
"a view name (e.g. ``my_view``): use that named view; prefix inspections are "
"then relative to each package's path in the view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:699
msgid "``false``: disabled (the default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:701
msgid ""
"spack:\n"
"  modules:\n"
"    view_relative_modules:\n"
"      use_view: my_view\n"
"    prefix_inspections:\n"
"      ./bin:\n"
"      - PATH\n"
"  view:\n"
"    my_view:\n"
"      root: /path/to/my/view\n"
"      projections:\n"
"        all: \"{name}-{hash}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:716
msgid ""
"The ``view`` key is discussed in detail in :ref:`Configuring environment "
"views <configuring_environment_views>`. With this configuration the "
"generated module for package ``foo`` would set ``PATH`` to include ``/path/"
"to/my/view/foo-<hash>/bin`` instead of ``/spack/prefix/foo/bin``. This is "
"useful for large deployments where view paths are simpler and more stable "
"than install-tree paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:721
msgid "Excluding packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:723
msgid ""
"The ``exclude`` and ``include`` keys control which packages get module files "
"generated. For example, to suppress module files for all packages compiled "
"with the system compiler except ``gcc`` and ``llvm`` themselves:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:726
msgid ""
"modules:\n"
"  default:\n"
"    tcl:\n"
"      include: [\"gcc\", \"llvm\"]  # include will have precedence over "
"exclude\n"
"      exclude: [\"%gcc@13\"]   # Assuming gcc@13 is the system compiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:734
msgid ""
"This suppresses module files for every package compiled with ``gcc@13``, "
"except ``gcc`` and ``llvm``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:736
msgid ""
"Combining ``exclude`` and ``autoload`` is safe: when a dependency has no "
"module file, ``autoload`` simply omits its load statement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:741
msgid "Maintaining Module Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:743
msgid ""
"Spack provides ``spack module tcl`` and ``spack module lmod`` commands for "
"managing module files. The available subcommands are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:750
msgid ""
"The ``refresh`` subcommand regenerates module files. Use the optional "
"``constraint`` argument to regenerate only packages matching a spec pattern. "
"Pass ``--delete-tree`` to delete the entire module tree before regenerating, "
"which is useful to handle a radical layout change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:758
msgid ""
"The ``rm`` subcommand deletes module files. By default, Spack asks for "
"confirmation before deleting; pass ``-y`` to skip the prompt in scripts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:765
msgid ""
"The ``find`` subcommand translates a spec into its module name, which is "
"useful for scripting:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:769
msgid ""
"$ spack module tcl find openmpi\n"
"openmpi/5.0.6-gcc-13.3.0-fjv7c7n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:774
msgid ""
"The spec must match one installation that has a module file, "
"or :ref:`several installations in the same module file<module-variants-"
"folding>`. Installations excluded from module files, with ``exclude`` or "
"``exclude_implicits``, are not counted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:779
msgid ""
"The ``setdefault`` subcommand marks a specific module version as the "
"default, creating a ``default`` symlink:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:787
msgid "Using Spack modules in shell scripts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:789
msgid ""
"Spack's shell functions and the ``module`` command are only available after "
"sourcing the appropriate setup file. Assuming Spack is installed in "
"``$SPACK_ROOT``, run the appropriate command for your shell:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:792
msgid ""
"# For bash/zsh/sh\n"
"$ . $SPACK_ROOT/share/spack/setup-env.sh\n"
"\n"
"# For tcsh/csh\n"
"$ source $SPACK_ROOT/share/spack/setup-env.csh\n"
"\n"
"# For fish\n"
"$ . $SPACK_ROOT/share/spack/setup-env.fish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:803
msgid ""
"To have Spack's shell support available at every login, add the appropriate "
"line above to your shell's startup file (``.profile``, ``.bashrc``, or "
"``.cshrc``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:808
msgid "``spack module tcl loads``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:810
msgid ""
"``spack module tcl loads`` (or ``spack module lmod loads``) generates "
"``module load`` statements for a spec. This is useful in scripts where "
"calling ``spack load`` directly would be too slow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:813
msgid ""
"The ``--dependencies`` flag includes load statements for all dependencies. "
"This is usually not needed since Spack packages embed RPATHs, but some "
"packages, in particular Python extensions, do not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:816
msgid ""
"$ spack module tcl loads --dependencies openmpi\n"
"# hwloc@2.11.2%gcc@13.3.0 arch=linux-ubuntu24.04-x86_64\n"
"module load hwloc/2.11.2-gcc-13.3.0-o7qgalw\n"
"# numactl@2.0.19%gcc@13.3.0 arch=linux-ubuntu24.04-x86_64\n"
"module load numactl/2.0.19-gcc-13.3.0-p7sk7en\n"
"# pmix@6.0.0%gcc@13.3.0 arch=linux-ubuntu24.04-x86_64\n"
"module load pmix/6.0.0-gcc-13.3.0-gwijn2q\n"
"# openmpi@5.0.6%gcc@13.3.0 arch=linux-ubuntu24.04-x86_64\n"
"module load openmpi/5.0.6-gcc-13.3.0-fjv7c7n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:828
msgid ""
"The output can be sourced directly using `process substitution <http://"
"tldp.org/LDP/abs/html/process-sub.html>`_:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:830
msgid "$ source <( spack module tcl loads --dependencies <spec> )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:838
msgid "Module Prefixes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:840
msgid ""
"On some systems, modules are automatically prefixed with a string. Use ``--"
"prefix`` to tell ``spack module tcl loads`` about it. For example, on a "
"system where modules are prefixed with the architecture string, without ``--"
"prefix`` the generated ``module load`` statement will not match the actual "
"module name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:844
msgid ""
"$ module avail\n"
"linux-SuSE11-x86_64/antlr/2.7.7-gcc-5.3.0-bdpl46y\n"
"\n"
"$ spack module tcl loads antlr\n"
"# antlr@2.7.7%gcc@5.3.0~csharp+cxx~java~python arch=linux-SuSE11-x86_64\n"
"module load antlr/2.7.7-gcc-5.3.0-bdpl46y\n"
"\n"
"$ spack module tcl loads --prefix linux-SuSE11-x86_64/ antlr\n"
"# antlr@2.7.7%gcc@5.3.0~csharp+cxx~java~python arch=linux-SuSE11-x86_64\n"
"module load linux-SuSE11-x86_64/antlr/2.7.7-gcc-5.3.0-bdpl46y"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../module_file_support.rst:857
msgid ""
"The same ``--prefix`` option is available for ``spack module lmod loads``."
msgstr ""

